 "topics": [
  {
   "topic": "Exact store failures covered by “refused” when recording cleanup failure",
   "members": [
    "r1.00",
    "r2.00",
    "r3.00"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "Does “refused” mean every unsuccessful store call, only a typed repository refusal, or some other closed set of failures?",
    "Does \"refused\" cover every failure of the store call that writes the cleanup-failed record, or only a particular refusal category; and what exact store outcomes belong to that category?",
    "Does “refused” mean every failed store call that would record the cleanup failure, or only a particular store result, and which exact store result triggers the retry rule?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows",
    "?"
   ],
   "id": "T1",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "answered",
    "quotes": [
     "- **Internal error.** `internal_error` is not a refusal, so \"a refusal writes\n  nothing\" does not hold for it. A failed store call changes no record (A18 rules 3-4); the\n  module returns it as `internal_error` and `module:surface` answers without\n  detail (A16 rule 7). When a request makes several store changes, those made\n  before the failed one stay: each is whole, and a run is derived from its\n  records (A14 rule 6; State 3, \"Run state after an element\").",
     "- **Stop required.** An unconfirmed sandbox cleanup is not a refusal: it is\n  the separate internal result `stop_required`, returned up the call chain\n  after the caller wrote its record; `module:surface` answers\n  `internal_error` — here, unlike any other internal error, that record was\n  written — and ends the process (State 3, \"Unconfirmed cleanup\"). If the\n  store refused that record, the caller's store failure is the internal error\n  instead, answered and followed by the same end of the process, and the next\n  start executes the element again (K-09, A03 rule 7)."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "The convention explicitly covers any failed store call and makes that store failure the internal error when the cleanup-failure record is not written.",
    "verified": true,
    "witness": {
     "outcomes": [
      "",
      ""
     ]
    },
    "blocking": false
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Startup replay scope, granularity, and durable identity for unrecorded run, admission, and explicit-try executions",
   "members": [
    "r1.01",
    "r2.02",
    "r3.01",
    "r3.02",
    "r3.03"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "Is automatic start-up re-execution limited to run elements recoverable from a Run, or does it also cover admission and explicit-try executions; if it covers the latter, what durable fact identifies the implementation and trial case after their record write failed?",
    "Which sandbox executions are automatically repeated at the next start: only function elements of durable runs, or also TrialExecutions performed by explicit try and admission; if trials are included, what durable record identifies the interrupted request, implementation, cases and actor to replay?",
    "For an admission execution whose cleanup-failure TrialExecution could not be stored, does startup retry anything automatically, or does admission wait for another implementation submission as A04 rule 2 specifies?",
    "If this sentence applies to admission, what durable work item is “the element”: only the trial case whose record failed, every case in that admission, or a newly started admission over the then-current corpus, and how is the pending admission identified?",
    "Does startup retry an explicit try operation, and if so where are its selected case list and requesting actor recovered from and what is written to TrialExecution.executed_by?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows",
    "?"
   ],
   "witnessed_by": [
    "r1.01",
    "r2.02",
    "r3.01",
    "r3.02",
    "r3.03"
   ],
   "id": "T2",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "judged_before",
    "quotes": [],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "P1 judged the same startup-replay question on the same recovery, admission, and explicit-try passages.",
    "precedent": {
     "id": "P1",
     "round": "round-188",
     "topic": "Recovery scope and durable state for admission and explicit-try TrialExecutions",
     "kind": "answered",
     "quotes": [
      "`surface.serve_kernel` runs the start in this order:\n  `installation.load_installation`; `bindings.check_installed_instances`;\n  `store.open_store`, which takes the lock,\n  checks links and removes temporary files; `sandbox.probe_sandbox`;\n  `effects.recover_in_flight_attempts`; `runs.recover_running_runs`, which first\n  names to `store.remove_run_spool` every ended run that no longer keeps its\n  spool (A14 rule 4), then derives again every run not ended — `running`,\n  `awaiting_approval` or `pending` — from its records (below, \"Recovering a\n  waiting point\"), rewriting a stored status or waiting point that lags\n  them, and advances every run that derivation leaves `running`; and only then\n  the first request (A03 rule 8, A14 rule 5, A18 rule 4).",
      "The\n  run is left `running`; the next start's recovery advances it from there with\n  that element concluded failed (or, if the store refused its record, executes\n  it again, K-09), so its dependants conclude `upstream_failed`\n  and independent branches go on (A13 rule 5, A14 rule 5); an interrupted admission has no verdict, so the next\n  submission of that implementation runs it again (A04 rule 2).",
      "Trying an implementation (State 0 action), by any agent,\n   executes an existing implementation on the cases of its contract version's\n   corpus that the request names, all of them when it names none, in corpus\n   order, records one TrialExecution per case and returns them. A request naming\n   an unknown case, a case of another contract version, or one case twice is\n   refused before anything executes, and so is a try on an empty corpus, as\n   `empty_corpus`. It computes no\n   verdict and activates nothing."
     ],
     "why": "Startup recovery advances only unfinished runs; an interrupted admission is rerun only by a later submission, while an explicit try is a request operation and has no startup-recovery path.",
     "units": [
      "?",
      "context"
     ],
     "unit_evidence": {}
    },
    "verified": true,
    "witness": {
     "outcomes": [
      "",
      ""
     ]
    },
    "blocking": false
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Behavior and external error signaling when the record write fails during startup recovery",
   "members": [
    "r1.02",
    "r2.03"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2
   ],
   "questions": [
    "What happens when this store failure occurs during start-up recovery, before the surface opens and there is no request to answer?",
    "When the failed record write occurs while startup recovery is executing an element before the surface opens, is there no response and startup simply fails, or is \"answered\" intended to require some other externally defined startup error channel?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows"
   ],
   "witnessed_by": [
    "r1.02",
    "r2.03"
   ],
   "id": "T3",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "answered",
    "quotes": [
     "`stop_required` from the sandbox, or `internal_error` from a store change,\nstops the start; a spool that cannot be removed does not (see Spool removal in\nConventions).",
     "A serving kernel; or a stopped start: the failing step and its reason on the\nhost's standard error, without secrets, and a non-zero process exit.",
     "- \"No partial start\" means only that the surface never opens: on any failing\n  step — a start check, a recovery step or a stopped start —\n  `capability:surface.serve_kernel` ends the process with a non-zero status,\n  and the lock taken in step 4 ends with the process, so a restart is never\n  blocked by it (A18 rule 1). Nothing written before the failure is undone:\n  every recovery change is its own store call and transaction (A18 rule 3), so\n  removed files and spools, attempts made `unknown` and runs advanced stay as\n  committed, and the next start runs the whole sequence again from them."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "During recovery the store internal error stops startup; no MCP entrance opens, and the host receives the failing step and reason on standard error plus a non-zero exit rather than an internal-error response.",
    "verified": true,
    "witness": {
     "outcomes": [
      "",
      ""
     ]
    },
    "blocking": false,
    "provenance": [
     {
      "quote": "- \"No partial start\" means only that the surface never opens: on any failing step — a start check, a recovery step or a ",
